    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. are there specific parts of the body where it's more common to see it in certain groups though? like, i read somewhere that for people with darker skin, it often shows up in places that don't get much sun, like the palms or soles of the feet. is that a reliable pattern to memorize for the exam?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While sun exposure is a major risk factor overall, melanoma can indeed occur in less sun-exposed areas. For darker skin, melanoma is often seen on mucosal surfaces, palms, soles, and under nails, which can be difficult for patients to monitor. However, memorizing this as a strict pattern might be misleading for an exam; it's more important to remember that darker skin can develop melanoma in *any* location. Always emphasize a comprehensive skin check to cover all areas.",
      "source": "target",
